How to get more physical therapy patients, in one sentence: close the three leaks between a referral and a first visit, then spend on being found. The leaks are a slow callback, an unanswered insurance question, and a referral nobody follows up. Most practices skip straight to the ads and pay to send patients into those leaks.

This guide goes through the three leaks in the order we fix them, then the campaigns that make sense once they are closed, with one clinic we work with as the example.

## Leak one: the callback that comes tomorrow

A patient fresh out of surgery is told to "call and schedule PT". They fill in your form at 7 pm. If your front desk calls back the next business day, they have often booked with the clinic that answered first. Post-op patients compare wait times, and the clinic that calls back in minutes wins without being any better at rehab.

Test it on yourself. Send your own website a form at 7 pm tonight and note when the call comes. That one number says more about your new-patient flow than your ad account does.

The fix is a callback within minutes on every form and every referral, evenings included, booked straight into your scheduling software, with a reminder that holds the slot. Our [physical therapy marketing page](/industries/physical-therapy/) describes how we set that up.

## Leak two: the insurance question nobody answered

Unclear coverage is one of the most common reasons an evaluation request never becomes a visit. The patient wants to know whether you take their plan and what it will cost. If the page does not say, they either ask by phone, which puts them back in leak one, or they leave.

Answer it before they ask. List the plans you are in network with. Give a self-pay price if you have one. Add one short screening question to the form so your team can check benefits before the callback, and the call becomes "you are covered, Tuesday at 9 is open" instead of "let me find out and get back to you".

## Leak three: the referral that leaks between the surgeon and your desk

A surgeon's office hands the patient a sheet and says "call them". Some patients call. Some mean to and do not. Nobody at either end knows which.

Make it easy and safe for the surgeon's office to send patients to you: a fast callback so their patient is booked before leaving the parking lot, a clear page for each type of surgery you rehab, and a short update on how their patients are doing. Be careful with gifts to referrers. Federal anti-kickback and self-referral rules limit what you can give someone who refers Medicare patients, so check with a healthcare attorney before any thank-you beyond a letter.

## What it looked like at one Denver clinic

The physical therapy clinic we work with in Denver was referral-dependent. It had a Google Ads account nobody had touched in a year, and web form leads were called back the next business day. All three leaks, in other words, and an ad account quietly spending into them.

We built a post-surgery rehab landing page with insurance screening, rebuilt the Google campaigns, and set up a callback within four minutes on every form. From February to July 2026 the clinic booked 2.4× more post-op evaluations on the same ad budget, cost per booked visit fell 41%, and two new referring surgeons found the clinic through the page. The figures are on our [results page](/results/), and the owner's verdict is there too:

> Four-minute callbacks did more than the new ads did.

Read that again before you raise an ad budget. The campaigns were rebuilt, and they helped. The callback is what the owner credits.

## Then, and only then, spend on being found

With the three leaks closed, every new enquiry is worth more, so paying for enquiries starts to make sense.

- **Direct-access campaigns.** Patients can come to physical therapy without a referral in every state, though the rules differ, so name the conditions you treat and say plainly whether a referral is needed where you are. Run local search and ads for those conditions and the post-op categories you rehab, not for "physical therapy near me". Our [paid advertising](/services/paid-ads/) page covers how we build them.
- **One page per surgery type.** A knee replacement page, a rotator cuff page, an ACL page, each answering the insurance question and ending in a form. These are the pages surgeons' offices and patients find.
- **Reviews after the plan of care.** Ask when the patient finishes, not after visit one, so the review describes the result.

## Keep the patients you have through their whole plan

More new patients is only half of a full schedule. The other half is patients who finish the visits they were prescribed. Measure your own drop-off first, visits attended against visits planned, by therapist and by condition, because the percentages quoted online rarely come from a published study. Then tell each patient at the evaluation how many visits they need and why, book ahead, and send reminders between visits. Patients who finished treatment years ago and were never invited back are worth a note too; our [patient reactivation](/services/patient-reactivation/) page explains how we run that.

## What to measure every Monday

- Minutes from form to callback, as a median and a worst case
- Evaluation requests, and how many became booked evaluations
- Booked evaluations that showed up
- Visits attended against visits planned

If the first number is in hours, fix it before anything else on this page. If it is in minutes and the schedule is still thin, then it is time to look at the campaigns.

